MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:5.5.t120-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:5.5.t147
REASONING=Main clause: **dedit** (independent, transitive active). Its subject is **lumen** ("this brilliance not only of intellect and letters but also of character and virtue gave this too..."), with **hoc** as the direct object anticipating the following *ut*-clause; the four genitives (ingeni, litterarum, naturae, virtutis) all depend on *lumen*, paired by **ac** and **atque**, and the two pairs are joined by the correlative **non solum ... verum etiam**, with *verum* taken as the coordinating conjunction linking *litterarum* (the immediately preceding item) to *naturae* (the following one). Ambiguity flagged: *hoc lumen* could alternatively be read together as the accusative object of *dedit* (with an unexpressed personal subject, "he gave this brilliance..."); I chose *lumen* as subject and *hoc* as the object anticipating the *ut*-clause, because *hoc ... ut* is Cicero's normal anticipatory idiom and the correlative *non solum ... verum etiam* brackets only the genitives, leaving *lumen* a single nominative head. Also a close call: *etiam* (t130) and *solum* (t124) are treated as adverbs modifying the main verb *dedit* (with *non* modifying *solum*), since no other target is licensed by the relation set. Result clause: **ut** subordinates to *dedit*; **esset** (dependent, linking verb) has *ut* as its unit-verb anchor, **domus** as subject (with **eadem** adjectival to it), **familiarissima** as predicate, and **senectuti** as a dative related to the verb. Relative clause: **quae** relates to its antecedent *domus* (relative pronoun) and is also subject of **favit** (dependent, intransitive, since *faveo* takes the dative); **adulescentiae** is its dative, **huius** a genitive depending on *adulescentiae*, and **prima** an adjective agreeing with *quae*.

